When you run the CALCGPT program on your calculator, it communicates with the chip using TIBASIC's built in link commands. These commands include the major get and send functions that are meant to communicate to other calculators via the link port in order to upload and transfer programs and files. The chip then makes HTTPS requests to the OpenAI or Telegram APIs over WIFI and relays the results back to the calculator screen. Setup is through a captive portal. The chip creates its own WIFI hotspot where you enter the credentials through a browser at 192.168.4.1. One computer science concept that shows in the project is gzip compression which is based on LZW. The TI-84 stores programs as binary tokens rather than plain text, so pushing the CALCGPT program from the chip to the calculator requires manually coding in the tokens which were done through https://www.cemetech.net/sc/. This is essentially the same thing a compressor does, but in this case it is done manually.
